    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. could you go into a bit more detail about how those financial instruments like the bills of exchange actually worked in practice? like what did the actual process look like for a merchant traveling a long distance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a merchant encountered a trusted banker along the Silk Road, they would deposit a signed receipt in a secure bank vault located in a major trade hub like Samarkand or Baghdad rather than transporting heavy gold or silver. The receipt listed the value of the goods they intended to sell at a future destination, effectively creating a credit instrument known as a bill of exchange. When the merchant finally reached their destination, they simply presented this original document to the local banker, who then paid them out in the currency of that region, meaning no valuable bullion ever traveled between the two cities. This system significantly reduced the risk of robbery, lowered transaction costs, and allowed for smoother, more complex long-distance commerce.",
      "source": "target",
